course: arts
lesson: 2
title: Reading music: notes, beats and rhythm
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Written music is a map of sound: notes sit on five lines called a staff (or stave), and the higher a note sits, the higher it sounds, like climbing a ladder.
explain: Notes sit both on the lines and in the spaces between them; a clef at the start tells you which line is which note, and silly sayings like FACE for the spaces help you learn them until you just know.
explain: Music also has a beat, a steady pulse like your heartbeat; bar lines cut the music into little boxes of equal beats, and the time signature at the start says how to count each box.
explain: Picture a class clapping "ta, ta, ti-ti, ta": saying a rhythm out loud before you play it helps your body feel the long and short sounds, and real musicians do it too.
explain: People name things differently: in Britain a one-beat note is a crotchet, in America a quarter note; Northerners in England say "nowt" for nothing, Geordies say "howay" for come on and "divvent" for don't, and many players call written music "the dots".
fact: Music is written on a staff, also called a stave, of five lines; each line and each space stands for a different note, and higher on the staff means higher in sound.
fact: Five lines give a good range of notes; for notes above or below the staff, short extra lines are added.
fact: In the treble clef the four spaces from bottom to top are F, A, C and E, and the lines are E, G, B, D and F, often remembered as "Every Good Boy Deserves Football".
fact: The treble clef is also called the G clef, because its curl wraps around the line for the note G; it tells you which line is which note.
fact: A crotchet (British) and a quarter note (American) are two names for the same note, which usually lasts one beat in 4/4 time.
fact: A rest is a written silence with a length you count, so the music keeps moving in your head while you stay quiet.
fact: A metronome is a ticking machine or app that keeps a steady beat; groups often rush when they get excited.
fact: A time signature is two numbers at the start: the top says how many beats are in each bar, the bottom says which kind of note gets one beat.
fact: 3/4 means three crotchet beats in each bar, which gives the swaying feel of a waltz.
fact: Bar lines split music into bars (called measures in America) with the same number of beats, and the music flows straight through them without stopping.
fact: A sharp raises a note by a semitone, the smallest step on a piano; a flat, which looks like a small b, lowers it by a semitone.
fact: Playing by ear means working out music by listening; many famous folk, blues and rock musicians mostly played that way, and many great musicians use both ear and reading.
fact: In the Kodaly method, named after the Hungarian composer Zoltan Kodaly, "ta" is said for a one-beat note and "ti-ti" for two half-beat notes.
exercise: How many lines does a music staff have? | Five | Four | Six | Ten
exercise: On a staff, what does a note sitting higher up mean? | A higher sound | A much louder sound | A longer, held note | A quieter sound
exercise: Emily hears the treble clef spaces spell FACE. Is that a joke? | No, it is a real memory trick for the notes F, A, C, E | Yes, teachers make it up to confuse children | Yes, FACE is just the name of a famous pop song | No, it means you play the notes with your face
exercise: Hao sees a curly "snail" at the start of his music. What is it? | The treble clef, which tells you which line is which note | A decoration that printers add to look nice | A sign that means play very slowly, like a snail | A mark that tells you to stop and rest
exercise: Jack says "crotchet", his cousin in Ohio says "quarter note". Who is right? | Both, they are two names for the same note | Only Jack, because music started in Britain | Only the cousin, because crotchet is a made-up word | Neither, the note is really called a minim
exercise: Zainab stops for a breather where "nowt" is written. What should she do at a rest? | Keep counting silently so she comes back in on time | Relax and stop counting until she feels ready | Play the next note early to fill the gap | Turn the page, because a rest marks the end
exercise: Tyler's class keeps speeding up. What helps them keep the beat? | A metronome that ticks a steady pulse | Playing louder so everyone can hear | Getting faster, because that shows they are improving | Each player keeping their own private speed
exercise: Olena sees 3 over 4 at the start. What does it mean? | Three crotchet beats in each bar | Play only three quarters of the piece | Play the third note of each bar four times | Three players and four singers together
exercise: What does a bar line do? | It splits the music into bars of equal beats | It tells you to stop playing and pause for a moment | It shows where the song finishes | It marks a very loud note
exercise: Ananya sees a "hashtag" before a note. What is it? | A sharp, which raises the note a semitone | A hashtag, so the song can be shared online | A flat, which lowers the note a semitone | A rest, which means stay silent
exercise: What does a flat do to a note? | Lowers it by a semitone | Raises it up by a semitone | Makes it last twice as long | Makes it much quieter to play
exercise: Callum says he plays "by ear" and does not need "the dots". What is fair to say? | Playing by ear is a real skill, and reading opens other doors | You cannot be a real musician without reading music | Reading music is pointless if you have a good ear | Only people who read music can play in bands
exercise: Bohdan thinks "ta, ta, ti-ti, ta" is baby talk. What is it? | Rhythm syllables that help you feel long and short sounds | A song written for babies in Hungary a long time ago | Nonsense the teacher uses to fill time | The names of the notes on the staff
exercise: Who is the Kodaly method named after? | Zoltan Kodaly, a Hungarian composer | A Japanese drum teacher who lived in Tokyo | The inventor of the metronome | A famous pop singer
exercise: Arjun asks why five lines and not ten. What is true? | Five lines give a good range, and short extra lines can be added | Ten lines would make every note sound much too high to sing or play | Five is the number of fingers, so it is the law | Music used to have ten lines but five broke off
test: Your mate reckons notes only ever sit on the lines, never in the gaps. Is he right? | No, notes sit on the lines and in the spaces too | Yes, the spaces are only there to look neat | Yes, but only in the bass clef | No, notes only sit in the gaps
test: What would happen if a player stopped counting during a long rest? | She might come back in at the wrong time | Nothing, because rests do not have a length | The rest would just get shorter on its own | All the other players would have to stop too
test: Kid in Auckland goes, "Sweet as, but what are all these little fences?" ("sweet as" means fine, great). What are the fences? | Bar lines that mark off the counting boxes | Rests where everyone in the band stops playing | Walls between different songs | Signs that the music gets louder
test: Your cousin says FACE is cheating and real musicians never use tricks. Which is true? | Musicians use memory tricks until the notes become automatic | Real musicians are banned from using any sayings or tricks at all | FACE only works on the piano | The trick gives the wrong notes on purpose
test: Dad says 3/4 means "you only play three quarters of the tune, then stop". What is wrong? | It tells you how to count, not how much to play | Nothing, that is exactly what it means | It means play the tune three times | It means four people play three notes
test: In a game, a player sees a sharp and plays the note lower. What should happen? | A sharp lifts the note a little, so it should go higher | That is right, a sharp always lowers a note a little | A sharp means skip the note entirely | A sharp means play the note twice
test: Grandad says, "If two countries use different words for one note, one of them is wrong." What is the best reply? | Different places can have correct names for the same thing | He's right, there must be one true name for each note, and the other is wrong | Only American names count in music | Only British names count in music
test: The class keeps rushing in the exciting bit. What is the most likely result? | The group falls apart instead of playing together | They sound better because faster is always better | Nothing changes at all | The music gets quieter by itself
test: Lad in Newcastle goes, "Howay man, reading music's for swots, me." ("howay" means come on). What is the fairest answer? | Playing by ear and reading are both useful skills | he's right, nobody needs to read music these days, man | He is wrong, nobody can play by ear | Only swots are allowed to play music
test: A cartoon shows the clef as a pretty curl the artist added for fun. What is the truth? | The clef tells you which line is which note | Clefs are only decoration and mean nothing | Clefs show how loud to play | Clefs tell you how many players there are
test: Why do teachers clap a rhythm before the class plays it? | Saying or clapping it first makes it easier to feel | Clapping warms up the instruments so they sound better | It stops the class from playing at all | It tells the teacher who is listening
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the time signature 3/4? | It indicates three crotchet beats within each bar | It indicates that three quarters of the composition shall be performed | It indicates that the third note shall be repeated four times | It indicates a fractional reduction in volume
exam: Identify the principal function of a rest as notated in a musical score. | The indication of a silence of determinate duration to be counted | The indication of an interval for the recuperation of the performer | The indication of the conclusion of the composition | The indication that the performer may proceed at will
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the terms "crotchet" and "quarter note". | They denote one and the same note in British and American usage respectively | They denote two notes of entirely different duration, the one twice as long as the other | The former is erroneous and the latter correct | The latter is erroneous and the former correct
exam: Determine the effect upon a note of a preceding sharp sign. | The raising of the note by one semitone | The lowering of the note by one semitone | The doubling of the duration of the note | The omission of the note entirely in performance
